= Contributing to BITwiki =

BITwiki should be easier to improve than it is to misunderstand.

You do not need to create a new article to contribute. In many cases, improving an existing page, adding a source, clarifying a technical explanation, recording a missing topic, or fixing navigation creates more value than adding another shallow page.

== Start here ==

Choose the smallest useful contribution:

{| class="wikitable"
! I want to… !! Best first action
|-
| fix an error || correct the specific statement and preserve/source the reason
|-
| add evidence || improve references, provenance, evidence notes, or source quality
|-
| make a page understandable || improve the Simple/Core entry without deleting needed technical detail
|-
| add technical depth || extend mechanisms, methods, implementation, standards, data, or limitations
|-
| connect knowledge || add justified relationships, categories, portal links, outlines, or cross-domain context
|-
| cover a missing topic || add it to [[BITwiki:Requested knowledge]] first unless you can create a coherent sourced page now
|-
| create a new page || research, source, model, write, verify, then publish a minimum coherent knowledge object
|-
| improve BITwiki itself || work in architecture, navigation, governance, validation, maintenance, or research queues
|}

== Before creating a new subject page ==

# Search for an existing canonical page, redirect, Book Matter fragment, or related page that already performs the job.
# Decide whether the material is a durable knowledge object, a navigation surface, project documentation, or a request/backlog item.
# Gather strong sources before writing factual claims.
# Choose semantic identity deliberately: Entity type, Domain(s), epistemic status, and provenance must not be guessed by a template default.
# Write enough coherent content that a reader can understand what the subject is and why the page exists.

'''Do not create generic shells merely to reserve titles.'''

A missing page is better represented as an explicit request than as a pseudo-article with one sentence and no evidence.

== Minimum coherent subject page ==

A new canonical subject normally needs enough signal to answer:

* '''What is it?''' — understandable lead and definition.
* '''Why does it matter?''' — scope/importance.
* '''How does it work or what are its core concepts?'''
* '''What are representative examples/applications?'''
* '''What is uncertain, disputed, contextual, or limited?''' when relevant.
* '''Where did the information come from?''' — traceable references/provenance.

Use [[BITwiki:Page format]] as the grammar, not as a requirement to fill every possible heading.

== Improve before inventing ==

A good newcomer path is often:

<pre>
read
→ notice a gap/error
→ make one sourced improvement
→ learn the corpus structure
→ make larger changes
→ create new knowledge only when needed
</pre>

This reduces duplicate pages, speculative schema, and low-information stubs.

== Simple → Core → Technical → Frontier ==

Accessibility and technical depth are both valid contribution targets.

* Improve the '''Simple''' orientation when a knowledgeable but non-specialist reader cannot enter the subject.
* Improve the '''Core''' layer when mechanisms/relationships are missing.
* Improve the '''Technical''' layer when formal, implementation, standards, tooling, data, or advanced details are absent.
* Improve the '''Frontier''' layer when research questions, uncertainty, disputes, or emerging evidence need explicit treatment.

See [[BITwiki:Readable depth and localization]].

== Human + AI contributions ==

AI assistance is allowed as tooling; it does not create authority by itself.

Human or agent contributions must:

* preserve source attribution and provenance;
* distinguish source material from synthesis and inference;
* not invent references, implementation state, consensus, or semantic identity;
* make uncertainty visible rather than smoothing it away;
* preserve meaningful authored/historical voice where canonicalization requires it;
* remain reviewable and reversible through normal version history.

Highly sensitive current political/religious material requires a more conservative posture; see [[BITwiki:Sensitive topic scope research]].

== Navigation contributions ==

A portal or category should exist because it helps readers traverse a real body of knowledge, not merely because a topic name exists.

Before creating one, ask whether a current Portal, Category, outline, list, query, or related-links section can do the job.

For categories, explain scope, includes, excludes/boundary, category role, parents/subcategories, important pages, and related navigation.

== Missing knowledge ==

Use [[BITwiki:Requested knowledge]] to record missing coverage when the research/write-up is not ready. A request is not a canonical article and should not be represented as one.

== BITwiki administration ==

Project-facing work belongs in the BITwiki namespace and related administration categories rather than subject Domains. Useful areas include:

* architecture and ontology;
* navigation and categories;
* epistemics/provenance;
* validation/quality;
* requested knowledge and coverage gaps;
* comparative wiki research;
* governance/security;
* templates/properties/automation;
* archive/V1 migration and reconciliation.

== External lessons ==

Wikipedia's contribution/newcomer systems are useful evidence because they separate small improvements, help/tutorials, first-article creation, requested articles, drafts/review, and maintenance work. BITwiki should adopt the clarity of those pathways without copying their procedural sprawl.

The maintained source list is [[BITwiki:Research references]].

